Day 241/250: Construct the Minimum Bitwise Array II

Problem Statement #3315

Problem Statment Explantion:

You are given an array nums consisting of n prime numbers. 
You need to construct an array ans of length n, where ans[i] is the minimum non-negative integer such that:
(ans[i] OR (ans[i]+1))==nums[i]
If no such integer exists, set ans[i] = -1.

Solution Approach:

Bitwise Logic:
The operation x OR (x+1) has a specific effect on the binary representation of a number: 
It finds the rightmost zero bit and sets it to 1, along with keeping all bits to its left the same.

Finding the Minimum
To find the smallest ans[i] for a given nums[i]:
Identify the Pattern: If nums[i]=2, no solution exists because x OR (x+1) results in an odd number (except for x=−1, which is not allowed). Since all other primes are odd, they end in ...011...1.
Trailing Ones: Every odd prime ends in a sequence of one or more 1s.
Example: 11 in binary is 1011.
Example: 31 in binary is 11111.
The Trick: To find the minimum ans[i], we need to change the most significant bit of the trailing block of 1s into a 0.
For 1011, the trailing ones start at the 2nd bit (value 2). Changing that bit to 0 gives 1001 (9).
Check: 9 OR 10⇒1001 OR 1010=1011 (11).

Here's the code:

import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public int[] minBitwiseArray(List<Integer> nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        int[] ans = new int[n];
        
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int x = nums.get(i);
            
            if (x == 2) {
                ans[i] = -1;
            } else {
                int k = 0;
                // Find the first 0-bit from the right
                while (((x >> k) & 1) == 1) {
                    k++;
                }
                // Flip the (k-1)-th bit from 1 to 0
                ans[i] = x ^ (1 << (k - 1));
            }
        }
        
        return ans;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity:	O(N⋅logM)
Space Complexity: O(N)

